WEBVTT

00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:16,020
And six ultrasound panels done on swollen lymph nodes in my groids, armpits, and neck looking

00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:18,080
for residual cancer last week.

00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:26,100
The scans came back as oversized nodes in groids, but the conclusion was benign in all,

00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,320
well, praise God for that.

00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,340
The oncologists want to biopsy the nodes in the groin in the armpits.

00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:39,040
If not cancerous, the oncologist wants to do surgery of my neck and remove the lymph nodes.

00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:45,420
As a precaution, and to test them, what are your thoughts?

00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:51,700
Well, in one sense, I would agree with your oncologist that, well, we don't know until

00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:58,180
we get a complete pathology report, maybe we take the tissue out and spit it onto a slide

00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:00,620
and look for cancer cells.

00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:06,780
There could be cancer cells there, but what are we going to do to remove all your organs

00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:07,780
to do that same thing?

00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:14,060
Well, you have to draw the line somewhere on how invasive you want to get.

00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:26,860
So if not invasive tests show a low possibility of cancerous tumors there, then I would be

00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:31,180
inclined to just say, okay, let's not to get too cut happy.

00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:38,980
Zero lymph nodes, they can be, so lymph nodes can enlarge when you're dealing with an infection.

00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:42,660
Lymph nodes can enlarge when you're dealing with a lot of toxins.

00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:48,300
Lymph nodes can enlarge when you're dealing with a lot of things that are going through them.

00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,380
So what goes through the lymph nodes?

00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:56,020
Anything that's cleared out of the tissues upstream from that lymph node.

00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,860
So things don't get cleared out of the tissues magically.

00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:05,940
They have to go through your open-ended lymph papillaries.

00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:16,380
So your lymph vessels pick up fluid and waste in the tissues and it drains down back to your

00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:24,940
venous system, which then goes through eventually the liver again and it's detoxified through

00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:27,260
the liver detox pathways.

00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:35,540
So that's how we get toxins out of our tissues, whether those toxins are from things that we're

00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:42,780
exposed to topically or whether things that get dumped from the bloodstream that we ingested

00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:54,140
or from surgical procedures that were distilled to that lymph node.

00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:59,060
Chemical procedures like chemotherapy that have to go through that, all those kind of things.

00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:04,580
So I mean it's up to you, I know there's a lot of people in the world that they just will

00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:09,180
just do everything that their oncologist says because they think that's the best chance

00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,180
to survive.

00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:16,700
They'll just go and have all those things removed and some people may, I'm not saying some

00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:20,180
people don't have good results with that but there's a lot of people that have bad results

00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:21,180
with that too.

00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:28,220
I think you have to make the decision to draw your line and have some sense of conservativeness

00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:31,580
in allowing the next procedures.

